{"title":"Convex set","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>Informally, a <b>convex</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Set\" title=\"Set\">set</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> is a set of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Point\" title=\"Point\">points</a> such that for any pair of points in the set, all the points between them (that is, on the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Line_segment\" title=\"Line segment\">line segment</a> that joins them) are members of the set as well.  Thus, every point in a convex set can \"see\" every other point in the set.  The <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Interior&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Interior (page does not exist)\">interiors</a> of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Circle\" title=\"Circle\">circles</a> and of all <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Regular_polygon\" title=\"Regular polygon\">regular polygons</a> are convex, but a circle itself is not because every segment joining two points on the circle contains points that are not on the circle.\n</p><p>More formally, a set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> in a space that allows for <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Addition\" title=\"Addition\">addition</a> of points and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Multiplication\" title=\"Multiplication\">multiplication</a> by <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Real_number\" title=\"Real number\">real numbers</a> (such as any real <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Vector_space\" title=\"Vector space\">vector space</a>) is said to be <b>convex</b> if for any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/d/7bda82ee8212aa2c03bc02d76fc28a5bda8412ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a,b\\in S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"59\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/e/17e380d7cf5a26fab725bb0c64b31ecfb8f4b69b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0\\le t\\le 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"72\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/5/7750ec156604dbf4eb4fb08abd36b5408debf980.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ta+(1-t)b\\in S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"132\" height=\"18\" />. \n</p><p>A set in a space that is not convex is called a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Concave_set&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Concave set (page does not exist)\">concave set</a>.  To demonstrate concavity is (in theory) relatively simple: one must find three points, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/e/50ec3017bd97bd3c28f8f4ddda626a6a76217c76.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a, b \\in S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"59\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/b/c2b4d9de05b4b7c003fc1451ace493a022b2593a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c \\not\\in S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"17\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> lies between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" />.  To prove that a set is convex, one must show that no such triple exists.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Convex_set&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Convex_hull\" title=\"Convex hull\">Convex hull</a></li></ul>\n<p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Convex_set&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018081616\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.016 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.029 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 70/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 271/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 1760/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.170      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.170      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:3311:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018081616 and revision id 23731. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}